Brick Breaker Game
A Python-based Brick Breaker game built with Pygame. This project
was completed as part of a software engineering lab assignment. The work
focused on correcting collision behavior, adding a proper end-game
state, implementing difficulty selection and replay, and adding gameplay
sound effects.
Student Details
- Name: Harshit Vaish
- SRN: PES1UG24AM116
- GitHub repository:
  https://github.com/harshitvaish94/49_brick-breaker
Project Overview
The player controls a paddle to keep the ball in play and destroy the
bricks. The game tracks the player's score and remaining lives. The
updated version adds clearer collision responses, win/loss handling,
selectable difficulty levels, replay through a menu, and sound effects
for important game events.
Features and Fixes
1. Directional Collision Detection
Problem: The original collision behavior reversed the ball's
vertical velocity for collisions regardless of which side of a paddle or
brick was hit. A side collision could therefore produce an incorrect
bounce.
Fix: The collision logic checks the ball's previous position
relative to the target rectangle and uses the collision side to decide
whether to reverse horizontal velocity (vx) or vertical velocity
(vy). When the previous position does not clearly identify the side,
the logic compares overlap amounts and resolves the collision along the
smaller-overlap axis.
Result: Side hits can reverse horizontal movement, while top/bottom
hits reverse vertical movement, making collisions more consistent.
2. Game Over, Win State, and Final Score
Problem: The game needed to handle the end of a round clearly
instead of continuing gameplay or closing immediately.
Fix: The game engine tracks game_over and result state. The
player loses when all lives are gone, and wins when all bricks have been
destroyed. Once the game ends, updates stop and an overlay displays the
outcome and final score.
Result: The end screen displays GAME OVER or YOU WIN!, the final
score, and an instruction to press a key to return to the menu.
3. Difficulty Selection and Replay
Fix: A menu lets the player choose Easy, Medium, or Hard. The
selected difficulty changes the ball speed and number of brick rows. A
new game engine is created for each new round, and a key press after the
end screen returns the player to the menu.
Controls: - 1 --- Easy - 2 --- Medium - 3 --- Hard - Esc ---
Quit - Arrow keys or A/D --- Move the paddle (depending on the
active input handling)
Result: Players can select a difficulty and start another round
without restarting the application.
4. Gameplay Sound Effects
Fix: Sound effects were added for brick hits, paddle hits, and
losing a life. Short sounds are generated in code rather than relying on
separate sound files. The sound helper checks whether Pygame's mixer is
initialized before attempting playback.
Result: The game provides audio feedback for these gameplay events,
while avoiding a crash if the audio mixer is unavailable.
5. Ignore Python Cache Files
A .gitignore entry was added to avoid tracking generated Python cache
files, keeping the repository focused on source and project files.
